------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party vendored wheels (offline wheelhouse)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Creator Engine ships an offline Python wheelhouse under
`validators/wheelhouse/` (runtime) and `validators/wheelhouse-dev/`
(development/test) so that the offline validator can be installed from
a fresh `git clone` without network access. Each wheel is distributed
unmodified under its upstream license; the upstream license text and
metadata are contained inside the wheel itself (typically under
`*.dist-info/LICENSE`, `*.dist-info/LICENSE*`, `*.dist-info/COPYING`,
or `*.dist-info/METADATA`).

The summary below is best-effort attribution derived from each wheel's
own metadata at the time of inclusion. The wheel's own metadata is
authoritative; in case of any conflict between this summary and the
wheel's `*.dist-info/METADATA`, the wheel's metadata governs.

Runtime wheelhouse (`validators/wheelhouse/`):

- PyYAML 6.0.2 — MIT License. Upstream: https://pyyaml.org/
- attrs 24.2.0 — MIT License.
- jsonschema 4.23.0 — MIT License.
- jsonschema-specifications 2024.10.1 — MIT License.
- referencing 0.35.1 — MIT License.
- rpds-py 0.22.3 — MIT License.

Development wheelhouse (`validators/wheelhouse-dev/`):

- build 1.3.0 — MIT License.
- iniconfig 2.3.0 — MIT License.
- packaging 26.2 — Apache License 2.0 OR BSD 2-Clause License
  (dual-licensed by the upstream project).
- pluggy 1.6.0 — MIT License.
- Pygments 2.20.0 — BSD 2-Clause License.
- pyproject_hooks 1.2.0 — MIT License.
- pytest 9.0.2 — MIT License.
